<!-- doc/src/sgml/undo.sgml -->

<chapter id="undo" xreflabel="UNDO Logging">
 <title>UNDO Logging</title>

 <indexterm zone="undo">
  <primary>UNDO logging</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> provides UNDO logging infrastructure
  that records the inverse of a change, so that the change can be reversed if
  the transaction that made it rolls back or is still in progress when the
  server crashes.  UNDO records are written into the WAL stream, so they are
  durable, replicated and archived like every other WAL record.
 </para>

 <para>
  UNDO is not a cluster-wide switch.  It is always-on infrastructure that is
  used only by the subsystems that write UNDO records.  The default
  <literal>heap</literal> table access method does not, and ordinary heap and
  index changes carry no UNDO overhead.  The one user of UNDO in
  <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> today is transactional filesystem
  operations (FILEOPS), which make the directory and file changes done by
  <command>CREATE DATABASE</command>, <command>ALTER DATABASE SET
  TABLESPACE</command>, <command>CREATE TABLESPACE</command> and
  <command>DROP TABLESPACE</command> roll back with the transaction.
 </para>

 <sect1 id="undo-fileops">
  <title>Transactional Filesystem Operations</title>

  <para>
   When a transaction creates, renames or writes a file, creates a directory,
   changes a file's mode or owner, truncates a file, or creates a link,
   FILEOPS makes the change and then writes a WAL record describing it and an
   UNDO record describing how to reverse it.  A change that fails is an
   ordinary <literal>ERROR</literal> and leaves nothing in the WAL.  If the
   transaction commits, the UNDO record is never used.  If it rolls back, the
   abort record carries the inverses, and the backend applies them, newest
   first, before <command>ROLLBACK</command> returns: a created file is
   removed, a changed mode is restored, a new database directory is removed
   with everything in it, and so on.  Removing a file or directory is not
   done immediately; it is deferred to commit and simply dropped on rollback.
  </para>

  <para>
   Between the change and its WAL record there is a short window in which a
   crash leaves the change in place with nothing to reverse it, as it would
   without FILEOPS.  Doing it the other way round, logging first, would turn
   every ordinary filesystem error into a <literal>PANIC</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   FILEOPS writes WAL and UNDO only when <xref linkend="guc-wal-level"/> is
   <literal>replica</literal> or higher.  At <literal>minimal</literal> it
   writes neither, and a crash in the middle of a transaction can leave a
   partly done filesystem change behind, as without FILEOPS.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="undo-crash-recovery">
  <title>Crash Recovery and Replication</title>

  <para>
   At the end of crash recovery, after redo, the startup process finds every
   transaction that wrote UNDO records but neither committed nor aborted
   before the crash, and applies its UNDO records before the server accepts
   connections.  Each checkpoint lists the UNDO chains of transactions in
   progress, so this includes a transaction whose UNDO records all precede
   the checkpoint redo starts from.  This step takes time proportional to the number of UNDO
   records those transactions wrote.  A prepared transaction is left
   prepared; <command>ROLLBACK PREPARED</command> applies its UNDO
   records later, and <command>COMMIT PREPARED</command> discards them.
  </para>

  <para>
   An UNDO record that cannot be applied, for example because the server no
   longer has permission to change the file it describes, is reported with a
   <literal>WARNING</literal> and is not retried, and the change it describes
   stays in place.  The end-of-recovery summary then also reports how many
   records could not be applied.
  </para>

  <para>
   A physical standby replays the WAL record of each filesystem change, and
   the inverses carried by each abort record, so it ends up with the same
   files as the primary.  Crash recovery replays them the same way.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="undo-configuration">
  <title>Configuration Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>undo_worker_naptime</varname> (<type>integer</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Time in milliseconds between runs of the UNDO discard worker.
      Default: <literal>10000</literal> (10 seconds).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>undo_max_wal_retention_size</varname> (<type>integer</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      If the WAL held back because of in-progress UNDO exceeds this size, in
      megabytes, the discard worker logs a <literal>WARNING</literal>.
      <literal>0</literal>, the default, disables the check.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>undo_batch_size_kb</varname> (<type>integer</type>)</term>
    <term><varname>undo_batch_record_limit</varname> (<type>integer</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A transaction's UNDO records are collected in memory and written as one
      WAL record when this many kilobytes (default <literal>256</literal>) or
      this many records (default <literal>1000</literal>) have accumulated,
      and at the end of the transaction.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="undo-space-management">
  <title>UNDO Space Management</title>

  <para>
   UNDO records live in the WAL stream as <literal>XLOG_UNDO_BATCH</literal>
   records; there are no separate UNDO files.  A checkpoint does not remove
   WAL that still holds the UNDO records of a transaction that is in progress
   or prepared, so a long-running or long-prepared transaction that wrote UNDO
   holds back WAL removal until it ends, much as a replication slot does.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="undo-monitoring">
  <title>Monitoring</title>

  <para>
   The server log shows UNDO being applied during crash recovery:
  </para>

<programlisting>
LOG:  UNDO recovery: rolling back transaction 742
LOG:  UNDO recovery complete: 1 transactions rolled back, 3 records applied
</programlisting>

  <para>
   and, if some records could not be applied:
  </para>

<programlisting>
WARNING:  UNDO recovery complete: 1 transactions processed, 0 records applied, 1 records could not be applied
DETAIL:  The changes those records describe were not rolled back.
</programlisting>

  <para>
   <application>pg_waldump</application> shows UNDO records with
   <option>--rmgr=Undo</option> and the filesystem changes themselves with
   <option>--rmgr=FileOps</option>.
  </para>
 </sect1>

</chapter>
